✅ What is `process.nextTick()` ?
====================================

    > Sir, process.nextTick is used to run a function immediately after current code execution.
    > It runs before event loop continues.
    > It has higher priority than setTimeout and setImmediate.

    If interviewer asks again:

    > It runs in microtask queue.
    > It executes before timers and I/O.
    > If we use too much, it can block event loop.

    ---

# ✅ What is `setImmediate()` ?
===================================

    > Sir, setImmediate is used to run a function after I/O operations.
    > It runs in check phase of event loop.
    > It executes after current event loop cycle.

    If interviewer asks difference:

    > process.nextTick runs first.
    > setImmediate runs later in event loop.

---

# ✅ Simple Difference (Very Important)

    You can say like this:

    > process.nextTick runs immediately after current function.
    > setImmediate runs in next event loop cycle.
    > nextTick has higher priority.
    > setImmediate is better for background tasks.

    ---

# ✅ Real Example (Your Product API Example)

    You can say:

    > In my project, after creating product, I send response to client first.
    > inserting into publish log and websocket I run inside setImmediate.
    > Because I don't want client to wait.
    > So background work runs separately.

    Very good practical answer 👌

    ---

# ✅ If interviewer asks “Why not nextTick?”

    You say:

    > nextTick runs immediately and can block event loop if heavy task.
    > So for DB calls or background work, setImmediate is safer.

    ---

Example : 
    console.log("Start");

    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log("setTimeout");
    }, 0);
    
    setImmediate(() => {
      console.log("setImmediate");
    });
    
    process.nextTick(() => {
      console.log("nextTick");
    });
    
    console.log("End");